{"call_id":"call_mc5yZlf6IrGKbE2D5uvSxJ5L","event_id":"ctc_0dbc3836272a32fc016aaa6caba4b887d2ad0fc6dcc6b60ce4","input":"const r=await Promise.allSettled([\ntools.exec_command({cmd:\"git-meta set --help; git-meta list:push --help; git-meta inspect --help; rg -n 'transcript|--file|stdin|path:|branch:|project|refs/meta|SQLite|database' $HOME/projects/git-meta/README.md | head -n 65; rg --files $HOME/projects/git-meta | rg 'spec|storage|target|cli|import' | head -n 40\",\"max_output_tokens\":4800}),\ntools.exec_command({cmd:\"python3 - <<'PY'\\nimport sqlite3\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\np=Path('$HOME/.codex/state_5.sqlite')\\nc=sqlite3.connect(p.as_uri()+'?mode=ro',uri=True)\\nprint(c.execute(\\\"select sql from sqlite_master where type='table' and name='threads'\\\").fetchone()[0])\\nPY\\nls -la .git-meta .git/meta* .git/git-meta* 2>/dev/null\",\"max_output_tokens\":2200})\n]);r.forEach(text);\n","name":"exec","session_id":"01a0a97e-9e6f-7410-ac4d-4cf241343851","source_line":331,"timestamp":"2026-09-16T10:17:19.448Z","type":"custom_tool_call"}
{"call_id":"call_mc5yZlf6IrGKbE2D5uvSxJ5L","event_id":"ctco_01a0a9b8-8def-7701-837c-bf18f4e7db94","output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"7d27e1\",\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":3201,\"output\":\"Set a string metadata value\\n\\nUsage: git-meta set [OPTIONS] <TARGET> <KEY> [VALUE]\\n\\nArguments:\\n  <TARGET>  Target in type:value format (e.g. commit:abc123)\\n  <KEY>     Key (can be namespaced with colons, e.g. agent:model)\\n  [VALUE]   String value (omit when reading from --file)\\n\\nOptions:\\n  -F, --file <FILE>            Read value from file\\n      --json                   Output as JSON\\n      --timestamp <TIMESTAMP>  Override timestamp (milliseconds since epoch, for imports)\\n  -h, --help                   Print help\\nPush a value onto a list\\n\\nUsage: git-meta list:push <TARGET> <KEY> <VALUE>\\n\\nArguments:\\n  <TARGET>  Target in type:value format\\n  <KEY>     Key\\n  <VALUE>   Value to push\\n\\nOptions:\\n  -h, --help  Print help\\nBrowse metadata keys and values\\n\\nUsage: git-meta inspect [OPTIONS] [TARGET_TYPE] [TERM]\\n\\nArguments:\\n  [TARGET_TYPE]  Target type to list (e.g. commit, change-id, branch, project)\\n  [TERM]         Fuzzy search term to filter keys/values\\n\\nOptions:\\n      --timeline  Show a weekly timeline graph of entries\\n      --promisor  List only promisor (not-yet-fetched) keys\\n  -h, --help      Print help\\n6:This is the spec and reference implementation for my proposal for a generic metadata system for Git projects. Specifically, to implement for storing metadata in Git projects for the [GitButler](https://gitbutler.com) tool, but there is interest in such a system for Jujutsu and the Git project generally.\\n8:This project's goal is to implement, benchmark and exercise a metadata system that is more flexible, scalable and useable than `git notes` that can be implemented by multiple systems.\\n16:I think the high level goals of this project were best laid out by Rodrigo Bovendorp's Jujutsu day talk in 2025, [Version-controlling metadata](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=46bV6KT0SsQ&list=PLOU2XLYxmsILM5cRwAK6yKdtKnCK6Y4Oh&index=4), so here is an overview of what he laid out in that talk:\\n20:Types of metadata that would be interesting to be able to attach to Git projects that existing tools in Git can't do well. For example:\\n25:  - prompts, transcripts, etc\\n38:  - project, commit, changeset, blob, path, line, byte?\\n65:Ok, so that's the landscape. This project is a proposal and reference implementation for a metadata solution for Git projects that attempts to nicely address most/all of this.\\n75:- Project (global space for the project as a whole)\\n92:Lists are meant to be generally append only collections, like comments or chunked agent transcriptions.\\n120:agent:transcript  ...\\n126:❯ git meta set path:src/git agent:rules -F GIT_AGENT.md\\n127:❯ git meta set path:src/observability agent:rules -F OBS_AGENT.md\\n128:❯ git meta set path:src/metrics agent:rules -F METRICS_AGENT.md\\n134:❯ git meta get path:src agent:rules\\n142:The list data type is for for cases like chunked transcripts or comments - where you would be maintaining a generally appended list of values for the key.\\n145:❯ git meta list:push branch:sc-feature-1 review:comments \\\"love it\\\"\\n147:❯ git meta list:push branch:sc-feature-1 review:comments \\\"like it\\\"\\n149:❯ git meta list:push branch:sc-feature-1 review:comments \\\"now I hate it\\\"\\n151:❯ git meta get branch:sc-feature-1 review\\n160:❯ git meta set:add path:src/metrics owners schacon\\n162:❯ git meta set:add path:src/metrics owners caleb\\n164:❯ git meta set:add path:src/metrics owners kiril\\n166:❯ git meta get path:src\\n169:❯ git meta set:rm path:src/metrics owners kiril\\n171:❯ git meta set:add path:src/metrics owners caleb\\n173:❯ git meta get path:src\\n179:For normal reading and writing of values, in this reference implementation, everything is kept in a SQLite database in the `.git` directory. Any large values are kept in Git as blobs and referenced from the values table. Small values are simply stored in the database.\\n195:However, we don't want to use Git as the direct data storage mechanism (ie, read and write directly to the Git object database) for several reasons. One is speed to read and write values. The other is that not every value will want to be transmitted.\\n197:So, we're thinking about this sort of like the local tuple database is an \\\"index\\\" or \\\"workding directory\\\"-like concept where data is manipulated, and then we \\\"commit\\\" (serialize) the data to Git when we want to transmit it. Then another user can fetch that Git data down and \\\"check it out\\\" (materialize) into their local working data store, merging it with any changes they've made.\\n201:It saves that new commit to `refs/meta/local/main` so it can be pushed. Other collaborators can fetch that head (into `refs/meta/remotes/main`) and \\\"materialize\\\" the data - adding any new or updated values into their working database and so on.\\n205:Serializing the data takes the targets, keys and values from SQLite and put them into a Git tree, generally of the format:\\n212:❯ git ls-tree -r refs/meta/local/main\\n221:Lists are handled slightly differently. Instead of `__value` as a blob at the termination of a key sequence, it is a tree called `__list` that has timestamped, hashed entries. So, for example, if you added two code owners to a path:\\n224:❯ git meta list:push path:src/metrics owners schacon\\n225:❯ git meta list:push path:src/metrics owners caleb\\n231:❯ git ls-tree -r refs/meta/local/main\\n238:This can also help chunk things like agentic transcripts, so instead of one large blob that may not work with github's 100M object limit, you can chunk it up, jsonl style or similar.\\n242:So, the general workflow would be that you would add metadata to your project's commits, changesets, paths or global scope and then when you push the code, you would simultaneously serialize the metadata and push that as well - either to the same repository or a seperate meta repository. This could be configured with fetch refspec, similar to normal Git branch usage.\\n247:2. You have started a metadata database, but someone else has too and pushed first.\\n253:This is pretty simple. We walk the tree pointed to by the head ref (`refs/meta/remotes/main` for example) and put all those tuples in our local database. We don't need to walk any farther in the commit history of the metadata.\\n287:As we'll see later, we will not assume that missing data means it should be deleted. One of our design decisions is to assume that purposeful removal of data is fairly rare and orphaned local data (data that is there but should not be) is not generally problematic. If we explicitly want to delete a value that has been propogated, we will have tombstone entries that inform the materialization process to remove a value if it exists in the local database.\\n291:One of the main driving points of this problem set is keeping prompts and transcripts for agentic work. As many solutions have already started finding out, this data adds up fast.\\n293:I've already seen projects trying to keep agentic transcript metadata where in less than 3 months, a codebase of 400 files and 2k commits with an entire Git code history clone of 14Mb has already accrued over 2.5Gb of transcripts data. A clone of this project expands from a 5Mb packfile without metadata, to a 500Mb packfile with metadata.\\n297:This project proposes two solutions, though only the first will probably generally be needed.\\n301:Getting every transcript for every commit in your entire codebase is probably not what you generally need. What you probably _generally_ want locally is recent transcripts and global or project wide data (codeowners, etc) and on-demand access to everything else.\\n307:Interestingly, as a benchmark for what that means, for the previous example of 500mb of compressed transcript data over 2k commits, a blobless clone would have been less than 2mb. The commits and trees are not the problem for a long, long while.\\n314:- Occasionally prune the tree itself to only be the first working set based on project settings and then do a full history walk to recreate a list of all the keys we've ever seen.\\n322:However, what if we want a value for some older target/key? Maybe we're doing a file blame and want to see which transcripts are available for all the last commits touching the file?\\n378:If you add a second metadata source, the local database becomes essentially the union of the data present in both.\\n382:One is to have private metadata accessible differently from public metadata. Perhaps your transcripts for a corporate open source project are in a private repository that only your employees can access, but everything else is in a public reference.\\n388:A fourth is to have data that is just for _you_ and not for your team or the public. Perhaps you're not comfortable sharing your prompts and transcripts, but you _do_ want to back them up or access them on other machines.\\n390:The difficulty is not really in reading the union of values from multiple refs into one database, the difficulty is knowing which new values should be written to refs bound for which remote reference.\\n392:If I add a transcript to a commit, how do I tell the system that this data is for my 'public', 'corporate' or 'personal' metadata source?\\n398:I feel like the simplest solution would be to have the tuple database source/destination agnostic and have some match filter that keys are compared against on serialization.\\n400:When there are multiple metadata destinations, filter rules can route keys to separate refs like `refs/meta/local/corporate`, `refs/meta/local/private`. The default destination is always `refs/meta/local/main`.\\n405:❯ tree .git/refs/meta\\n417:I'm including a working Rust reference implementation with the CLI surface that was in the examples. You can compile this project with `cargo build` and test it out on any Git repository.\\n431:Not much of this will be in scope for this project (though I may do some working implementations for testing). This project is only about deciding on the format and working semantics.\\n433:Once we've decided the final implementation details, we will be incorporating this into the [gitbutler](https://github.com/gitbutlerapp/gitbutler) project. The more formal, detailed spec is found in the [spec](/spec) folder.\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/blog/large-files-and-object-storage/index.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/assets/style.css\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/workflow.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/remotes.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/api.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/auto-sync.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/output.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/cli.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/overview.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/serialize-filters.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/storage.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/standard-keys.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/implementation/pruning.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/exchange.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/targets.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/materialization.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/lists.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/strings.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/exchange-format/sets.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/docs/spec/index.html\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/content/large-files-and-object-storage.md\\n$HOME/projects/git-meta/spec/implementation/p